  "summary": "Today's health-tech landscape reflects several converging trends reshaping the industry. Regulatory scrutiny is intensifying on multiple fronts: the FDA is developing guidance for generative AI in medical devices, paused a Regenxbio gene therapy trial due to safety concerns, and faces an FTC challenge to Amgen's patent strategy. Meanwhile, workforce challenges are mounting, with nurse practitioner trainees struggling to find clinical mentors and many paying out-of-pocket for supervision. On the clinical innovation side, notable wins include Moderna and Merck's cancer vaccine progress despite logistical hurdles, Roche acquiring an obesity drug that preserves muscle mass, and FDA authorization for Avulux migraine lenses, while emerging areas like metabolic psychiatry gain traction. Healthcare organizations are adopting AI tools for clinical communication and metabolic care despite persistent privacy concerns, and payers are shifting toward prevention-focused strategies to combat waste and abuse. Finally, consumer health products face scrutiny, with Oura wearables facing accuracy lawsuits and emerging data showing SNAP restrictions modestly reduced soda purchases by twelve percent.",
